WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.434
The former governor of Alaska,

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:01.968
Sarah Palin.

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.103
She has lost her bid

00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:03.870
to win a seat

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.572
in the House of Representatives.

00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:06.039
Now, Palin's

00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:08.141
defeat is the latest political setback

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:08.575
for someone

00:00:08.575 --> 00:00:10.610
who was once considered a rising star

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.679
of the Republican Party.

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:13.480
Joining us to discuss

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:14.647
this is Julian Zelizer.

00:00:14.647 --> 00:00:16.016
He is a CNN political analyst

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:17.017
and historian,

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:17.784
as well as a professor

00:00:17.784 --> 00:00:18.785
at Princeton University.

00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:20.220
Julian, thanks so much for joining us.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.688
You just wrote a very interesting

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:23.156
opinion piece on CNN.com.

00:00:23.156 --> 00:00:24.090
And you make the case

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.493
that Sarah Palin has lost the battle

00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:27.660
but won the war.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:28.928
You say, quote, Palin's

00:00:28.928 --> 00:00:30.530
political legacy will endure.

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:31.231
The former

00:00:31.231 --> 00:00:33.333
Alaska governor was a pivotal figure

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:34.334
in mainstreaming

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:36.069
a new style of Republican politics

00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:37.971
that ignored traditional guardrails.

00:00:37.971 --> 00:00:39.205
Her brand of conservative

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:41.207
popular populism, weaponized social

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:42.375
and cultural outrage,

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:44.377
and mobilized working class Americans.

00:00:44.411 --> 00:00:45.812
Palin, in short,

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:47.213
mastered Trumpism

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:47.947
long before

00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:48.948
Donald Trump ever

00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:50.984
set foot in the White House.

00:00:50.984 --> 00:00:51.518
So, Julian,

00:00:51.518 --> 00:00:51.985
how is it

00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:53.953
that the person who arguably unleashed

00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:54.888
this phenomenon

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:56.222
may now be out of politics?

00:00:57.791 --> 00:00:58.625
Well, she hasn't

00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:00.693
found the kind of electoral success

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:01.928
that she's looking for.

00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:03.496
And it's unclear

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:05.498
the kind of political legs

00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:08.134
that this kind of Republicanism has.

00:01:08.134 --> 00:01:10.070
That said, it's pretty clear

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:11.971
how the Republican Party has changed.

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:14.707
It didn't all start in 2017.

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:16.676
And she was one of the figures

00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:17.610
I talk about.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.512
Newt Gingrich is being another

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:20.980
who gradually changed

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.083
what the Republican Party was

00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:26.386
and opened the door to a very fierce

00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:29.355
kind of reactionary populism

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:31.391
that has been prevalent

00:01:31.391 --> 00:01:33.526
during the last few years for sure,

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:36.362
but started a lot earlier.

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:39.532
Do you think this rejection of Palin

00:01:39.532 --> 00:01:40.500
by Alaska

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.936
voters should serve as a warning sign

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:44.370
to other Republicans?

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:45.271
In the same vein,

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:46.372
like the former president

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:48.241
who has just launched his

00:01:48.241 --> 00:01:49.609
his, he's just announced that he's

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:50.844
running for president in 20, 24.

00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:53.847
It might, but I think that I

00:01:53.847 --> 00:01:54.881
would underestimate

00:01:54.881 --> 00:01:58.218
how deep this kind of politics is

00:01:58.218 --> 00:01:59.419
in terms of how rooted

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:01.287
it is in the Republican Party.

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:03.456
Parties don't change overnight.

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:04.557
Parties won't change

00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:06.626
because of one midterm election.

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:08.461
My guess is the fact they control

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:10.630
the House will give them some sustenance.

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:11.531
And I think

00:02:11.531 --> 00:02:13.700
it's going to take a series of election

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.235
losses, meaning

00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:16.736
many years of losing,

00:02:16.736 --> 00:02:20.173
not just in one seat, but for Republicans

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:21.941
to start to believe

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:23.510
that they have to change their ways.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:24.544
I don't think the party is

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:25.778
feeling that way right now.

00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:28.181
So the brand that Gingrich, Palin

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:28.648
and Trump

00:02:28.648 --> 00:02:29.849
all embody,

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:31.017
I think is still alive

00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:32.452
and well in the party.

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:34.554
So even after this blow to Republicans

00:02:34.554 --> 00:02:35.255
in the midterms,

00:02:35.255 --> 00:02:36.923
you don't think that they're going to

00:02:36.923 --> 00:02:37.557
to change?

00:02:37.557 --> 00:02:38.158
You don't think that

00:02:38.158 --> 00:02:39.325
they're going to be able to to

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:40.293
cast Trump aside?

00:02:41.861 --> 00:02:43.196
No, I think they might

00:02:43.196 --> 00:02:44.631
offer a different version

00:02:44.631 --> 00:02:46.232
of what we call Trumpism

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:48.368
and maybe that's a Governor DeSantis,

00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:49.636
or maybe that's

00:02:49.636 --> 00:02:50.637
the former president

00:02:50.637 --> 00:02:52.105
trying to position himself

00:02:52.105 --> 00:02:52.872
in a different way.

00:02:52.872 --> 00:02:53.740
But as the story

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.742
you started with suggests,

00:02:55.742 --> 00:02:57.076
that's going to be very hard.

00:02:57.076 --> 00:02:58.411
But but I don't think the party

00:02:58.411 --> 00:02:59.112
is going to change

00:02:59.112 --> 00:03:02.949
as quickly as some observers believe

00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:04.050
All right, Julian Zelizer,

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:04.984
we'll be very interesting

00:03:04.984 --> 00:03:05.351
to see

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:06.786
what happens both to the party

00:03:06.786 --> 00:03:08.821
and to to Sarah Palin herself.

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:09.622
Julian Zelizer,

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:10.456
thank you so much for your time.

